<!-- BAND 5: COMPARISON — CMP, FLUTTER, REACT NATIVE -->
<section id="compare" class="prose-section prose-section-alt">
  <div class="site-container">
    <div class="prose-header-center">
      <p class="prose-eyebrow">Framework Comparison</p>
      <h2 class="prose-h2">Positioned beside CMP, Flutter, and React Native</h2>
      <p class="prose-lead">
        Keep real native controls when users expect OS fidelity, and switch to a GPU canvas when your product demands custom pixels &mdash;
        without a JavaScript engine or desktop JVM.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="compare-caption" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <caption id="compare-caption">Architectural comparison: Osprey Native vs. Compose Multiplatform (CMP), Flutter, and React Native</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Capability</th>
            <th scope="col" class="col-osprey">Osprey Native</th>
            <th scope="col">Compose Multiplatform (CMP)</th>
            <th scope="col">Flutter</th>
            <th scope="col">React Native</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Rendering model</th>
            <td class="col-osprey"><strong>Both:</strong> real native controls (SwiftUI, WinUI, Android) <strong>OR</strong> FerroUI / GPUI GPU canvas</td>
            <td>Skia canvas for shared UI (native views require platform interop wrappers)</td>
            <td>Impeller / Skia canvas only (framework-drawn widget copies)</td>
            <td>Native platform views only (custom GPU scenes need native modules)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Runtime &amp; compilation</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">LLVM AOT native machine code everywhere + WebAssembly on web; no JS engine, no JVM</td>
            <td>Kotlin/Native on iOS, JVM on desktop &amp; Android, Wasm/JS on web</td>
            <td>Dart AOT on mobile &amp; desktop, JS/Wasm on web</td>
            <td>JavaScript engine (Hermes) + JSI / native bridge</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Reactivity</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">Compiler-derived <code>Signal&lt;T&gt;</code> &amp; keyed regions from the ground up</td>
            <td>Compose Snapshot state + compiler recomposition</td>
            <td>Widget tree rebuilds + third-party state packages</td>
            <td>React hooks + virtual DOM reconciliation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language &amp; syntax</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">Default Osprey, ML, Python, TypeScript flavors (Dart coming soon; Kotlin considered)</td>
            <td>Kotlin only</td>
            <td>Dart only</td>
            <td>JavaScript / TypeScript only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Desktop &amp; Windows</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">Genuine SwiftUI (macOS), genuine WinUI MSIX (Windows), or FerroUI GPU canvas</td>
            <td>Skia drawn inside a JVM / Swing desktop window</td>
            <td>Custom canvas inside Win32 / macOS shell</td>
            <td>Community-maintained Windows &amp; macOS forks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Accessibility &amp; AI agents</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">Enforced typed <code>Accessible</code> tree (<code>semantics</code> / <code>act</code>) + AccessKit; tested by label on every build</td>
            <td>Opt-in semantics modifiers per composable</td>
            <td>Opt-in <code>Semantics</code> widgets</td>
            <td>Opt-in accessibility props per view</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Automated UI testing</th>
            <td class="col-osprey">Headless <code>@osprey/uitest</code> by accessible label + algebraic effect mocks (no window/simulator)</td>
            <td>Compose UI test rules (JVM / platform runner)</td>
            <td>Widget tests + <code>integration_test</code></td>
            <td>Jest + Detox / Appium</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="cmp-highlights">
      <h3 class="prose-h3">Where Osprey Native pulls ahead of Compose Multiplatform (CMP)</h3>
      <div class="prose-grid-3" style="margin-block-start: 1.25rem;">
        <article class="prose-card">
          <h4 class="prose-h3">True native controls on Apple &amp; Windows</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            CMP paints shared UI onto Skia and hosts desktop in a JVM/Swing shell. Osprey Native renders real SwiftUI on macOS/iOS
            and real WinUI on Windows &mdash; or switches to FerroUI's GPU canvas whenever you want identical pixels.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="prose-card">
          <h4 class="prose-h3">No JVM on desktop, no Kotlin lock-in</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            Every native target compiles AOT to machine code with a zero-serialization shared-memory ABI. Teams write in Default Osprey,
            ML, Python, or TypeScript flavor today, with Dart flavor coming soon.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="prose-card">
          <h4 class="prose-h3">Structural accessibility for AI agents</h4>
          <p class="prose-body">
            UI machines make roles and live labels structural instead of optional modifiers, so screen readers, headless
            <code>@osprey/uitest</code> runs, and AI agents share one verified interaction surface.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

